Games are full of maths on small types: positions, velocities, colours. C++ lets those types use real operators, so `pos += vel * dt;` reads like the formula:

```cpp
struct Vec2 {
    float x = 0, y = 0;

    Vec2& operator+=(Vec2 o) { x += o.x; y += o.y; return *this; }
};

Vec2 operator+(Vec2 a, Vec2 b) { return {a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y}; }
Vec2 operator*(Vec2 v, float k) { return {v.x * k, v.y * k}; }
```

`operator+` and `operator*` take their arguments **by value** (a `Vec2` is just two floats) and return a **new** `Vec2`. `operator+=` is a member: it changes the object on its left and returns a reference to it. That is the standard pattern: `+` builds a new value, `+=` changes one in place.

