When the fox is hurt, the health bar, a sound and the camera shake all need to react. `Health` shouldn't know about any of them. Instead it keeps a list of **callbacks**: `std::function<void(int)>` holds any callable that takes an `int`, usually a lambda.

```cpp
struct Health {
    int current = 3;
    std::vector<std::function<void(int)>> onChanged;   // the listeners

    void damage(int amount) {
        current -= amount;
        for (auto& listener : onChanged) listener(current);
    }
};
```

Listeners subscribe with `health.onChanged.push_back([](int hp) { ... });`. A lambda that captures with `[&]` keeps references to local variables, so those variables must outlive the callback.

