Moving 2 pixels *per frame* ties speed to the frame rate. The fix: measure how long the last frame took, **delta time** or `dt`, in seconds, and write every speed **per second**: `x += speed * dt;`

```cpp
auto previous = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
while (running) {
    auto now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    float dt = std::chrono::duration<float>(now - previous).count();   // seconds
    previous = now;
    update(dt);
    render();
}
```

`std::chrono::steady_clock` never jumps backwards, unlike the wall clock. Engines hand you the same number: Godot passes `delta` to `_process`, Unreal passes `DeltaTime` to `Tick`.

At 60 fps `dt` is about 0.0167 s and at 30 fps about 0.033 s. The slow machine takes bigger steps, but fewer of them, so both cover `speed` units every second: distance = speed × time, once per frame.

