The simplest collider is an **AABB**, an *axis-aligned bounding box*: a rectangle that never rotates, stored as its min and max corners. Two AABBs overlap only if they overlap **on both axes**:

```cpp
struct Box { float minX, minY, maxX, maxY; };

bool overlaps(Box a, Box b) {
    return a.minX < b.maxX && a.maxX > b.minX &&   // overlap on x
           a.minY < b.maxY && a.maxY > b.minY;     // and on y
}

int main() {
    Box fox{0, 0, 1, 1};
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << overlaps(fox, {0.5f, 0.5f, 2, 2}) << '\n';   // true
    std::cout << overlaps(fox, {1, 0, 2, 1}) << '\n';         // false: edges only touch
}
```

It prints `true` and `false`. Strict `<` and `>` mean boxes whose edges merely touch do not count, which lets the fox stand *on* a platform without being "inside" it. A `Box` is four floats, so it is passed by value.

