Here is a minimal version, used in the snippets that follow. Each frame the game calls `update(dt)` on every object, which calls it on each of its components:

```cpp
// gameobject.h
#pragma once
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>
#include "vec2.h"

struct GameObject;

struct Component {
    virtual ~Component() = default;
    virtual void update(GameObject& owner, float dt) = 0;
};

struct GameObject {
    std::string name;
    Vec2 position;
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Component>> components;

    explicit GameObject(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) {}

    template <class T>
    T& add() {
        components.push_back(std::make_unique<T>());
        return static_cast<T&>(*components.back());
    }

    template <class T>
    T* get() {
        for (auto& c : components)
            if (auto* found = dynamic_cast<T*>(c.get())) return found;
        return nullptr;
    }

    void update(float dt) {
        for (auto& c : components) c->update(*this, dt);
    }
};
```

The components have different types, so the object stores **pointers to the base class**, and `std::unique_ptr` makes the object their single owner: they are deleted with it. `add<Mover>()` creates one and returns a reference to it; `get<T>()` finds one with `dynamic_cast`, which returns `nullptr` when a component is not a `T`.

