A **pool** keeps objects that are not in use and hands them out again instead of creating new ones:

```cpp
// pool.h
#pragma once
#include <cstddef>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>

template <class T>
class Pool {
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<T>> all;   // owns every object ever created
    std::vector<T*> freeList;              // the ones not in use right now

public:
    T* get() {
        if (!freeList.empty()) {
            T* item = freeList.back();
            freeList.pop_back();
            return item;                   // reuse: no allocation
        }
        all.push_back(std::make_unique<T>());
        return all.back().get();
    }

    void release(T* item) { freeList.push_back(item); }

    std::size_t created() const { return all.size(); }
};
```

`get` reuses a free object if there is one and creates a new one only when the pool is empty. `release` puts an object back. After a short warm-up the game stops allocating bullets entirely. Each object lives in its own `unique_ptr`, so a `T*` stays valid while `all` grows.

Engine code goes further: one preallocated block of slots, with handles that notice when a slot has been reused (see the Game Engine Programming course).

