Computer random numbers are **pseudo-random**: a formula turns a **seed** into a long sequence that looks random. Same seed, same sequence, same world. That gives you daily levels every player shares, replays, and bug reports that say *"seed 1234 crashes"*.

`std::mt19937` from `<random>` is fully specified by the C++ standard (it even states that the 10,000th number from the default seed is 4123659995), so a seed gives the same raw numbers with every compiler. The **distributions**, such as `std::uniform_int_distribution`, are not specified that exactly, so different standard libraries may turn those numbers into different results. For worlds that must regenerate identically everywhere, map the raw numbers yourself:

```cpp
struct Rng {
    std::mt19937 engine;
    explicit Rng(std::uint32_t seed) : engine(seed) {}

    // A whole number from min up to (not including) max.
    int range(int min, int max) {
        return min + static_cast<int>(engine() % static_cast<std::uint32_t>(max - min));
    }
};
```

The `%` makes some values very slightly more likely than others, which is fine for a level generator.

