#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <random>

struct Rng {
    std::mt19937 engine;
    explicit Rng(std::uint32_t seed) : engine(seed) {}
    int range(int min, int max) { return min + static_cast<int>(engine() % static_cast<std::uint32_t>(max - min)); }
};

int main() {
    Rng before(42);                 // version 1: two trees
    int treeA = before.range(0, 100);
    int treeB = before.range(0, 100);

    Rng after(42);                  // version 2: same seed, a rock added in between
    int treeA2 = after.range(0, 100);
    int rock = after.range(0, 100);
    int treeB2 = after.range(0, 100);

    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << (treeA == treeA2) << '\n';
    std::cout << (rock == treeB) << '\n';
    std::cout << (treeB == treeB2) << '\n';
}

steps.body:
Save **data**, not live objects: the seed, the fox's position and health, item ids in the inventory, which chests are open. Rebuild everything else on load: the generated map, textures, pointers between objects (an address means nothing in the next run).

Three habits prevent pain:

- Put a **version** number in every save, and upgrade old versions when loading.
- Use a plain format: one `key value` per line, written with `<fstream>` and read back with `>>`, is enough for a small game.
- Write to a temporary file, then replace the old save in one step, so a crash mid-save cannot leave a half-written file:

```cpp
void save(const SaveData& s) {
    {
        std::ofstream out("save.tmp");
        out << "version " << s.version << '\n'
            << "seed " << s.seed << '\n'
            << "health " << s.health << '\n';
    }                                               // the file is closed here
    std::filesystem::rename("save.tmp", "save.txt");
}
```

On Linux and macOS, `rename` swaps the new file in with a single operation.

